.TH QTPASS 1 "September 2026" "QtPass 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qtpass \- graphical front end for pass, the standard Unix password manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qtpass
.RI [ "Qt options" ]
.RI [ "search text" ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B QtPass
shows a password store as a tree, decrypts an entry when it is selected
and lets you add, edit, delete, move and share entries. The store is the
same directory that
.BR pass (1)
uses, usually
.IR ~/.password-store :
one GnuPG-encrypted file per password, encrypted to the keys listed in the
nearest
.I .gpg-id
file, optionally kept under Git. QtPass does no encryption itself; it runs
.BR gpg (1),
and either
.BR pass (1)
or
.BR git (1)
directly (the \(lqnative\(rq backend, which needs neither pass nor a shell).
.PP
Features, all switched on or off in
.BR Configuration :
.IP \(bu 3
Several password stores side by side as profiles, each with its own Git
settings and, when the store demands it, signing key for
.IR .gpg-id .
.IP \(bu 3
Per-folder recipients: choose who can decrypt a folder and re-encrypt it
for them.
.IP \(bu 3
Templates that turn
.I key: value
lines of an entry into fields; field names can be renamed in place.
.IP \(bu 3
Built-in one-time passwords (TOTP per RFC 6238, SHA-1/256/512, Steam
Guard) from an
.I otpauth://
URI or a
.I TOTP:
line; the secret is never shown.
.IP \(bu 3
A password generator using the system's random source or
.BR pwgen (1),
with a choice of character sets.
.IP \(bu 3
Clipboard modes (always, on demand, never) with a clearing timeout, and
options to hide the password or the whole entry against shoulder surfing.
.IP \(bu 3
Full-text search through decrypted entries with
.BR "pass grep" ,
QR codes for a value, a system tray icon, and a menu bar that is off by
default (Ctrl+M).
.SH ARGUMENTS
Any non-option argument is used as the initial search text: the tree is
expanded and filtered to it, and an exact match is opened. When
QtPass is already running, the arguments are handed to that instance
instead of starting a second one, and its window is raised.
.SH OPTIONS
QtPass has no options of its own. The standard Qt options apply, among
them
.BR \-platform ,
.BR \-style ,
.BR \-qwindowgeometry ,
.BR \-qwindowtitle ,
.BR \-reverse
and
.BR \-session .
Options are not passed on to a running instance.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GNUPGHOME
Passed through to
.BR gpg (1)
unless a GnuPG home directory is set in Configuration, which then wins.
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
Used by
.BR git (1)
for SSH remotes. QtPass probes
.B gpgconf
for the agent's socket when the variable is unset; an explicit path can be
set in Configuration.
.TP
.B QT_LOGGING_RULES
.B QT_LOGGING_RULES=qtpass.debug=true
prints every command QtPass runs, with its arguments and exit status, to
standard error. Useful for bug reports; what is fed to those commands
(passwords, passphrases) is not logged.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.password-store
The default password store; another directory can be chosen per profile.
.TP
.I ~/.config/IJHack/QtPass.conf
Settings on Linux and BSD. macOS uses
.IR ~/Library/Preferences/com.IJHack.QtPass.plist ,
Windows the registry key
.IR HKEY_CURRENT_USER\eSoftware\eIJHack\eQtPass .
.TP
.I .gpg-id
Per folder, the recipients an entry below it is encrypted to; managed with
the Users dialog.
.TP
.I .gpg-id.sig
The signature pass requires when
.B PASSWORD_STORE_SIGNING_KEY
is in use; QtPass writes it with the profile's signing key.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a normal exit, also when the arguments were handed to an instance
that was already running.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pass (1),
.BR gpg (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR pwgen (1)
.PP
